{
  "id": "deformation_beneficiary",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "comment": "Emit seeds/deformation_beneficiary.json \u2014 WHOSE MANIFOLD DOES THIS DEFORMATION SERVE?\n\n`COMMUNICATION_DEFORMATION_ALIGNMENT.md` found that the substrate confirms the\npapers' structural claim (communication and manipulation are one operation, no\nsecond primitive) and cannot represent the axis the ethics sits on (whose manifold\nthe deformation serves). It also found the fix I first proposed was wrong: a\nbeneficiary field on EVERY deformation is ill-posed, because forced deformation is\nthe ordinary container relation and most of it has no author. Gravity serves\nnobody, and a field that must be filled for gravity gets filled with noise.\n\nSo the order is: the GOAL-BEARING TEST first, the two fields only where it passes.\n\n## The goal-bearing test\n\nA frame is goal-bearing iff it holds a represented preference over its own\ncontinuations \u2014 which in this substrate is exactly a TUBE: `future_manifold_target`\nplus `future_manifold_bounds`, the thing that makes some continuations better than\nothers FOR IT. Gravity has no tube. This is not a type-name check and not a list of\ngoal-ish node types; it is the structural signature the manifold machinery already\nuses, and the same field `feasible_selection` breaks ties on.\n\n## The two fields, and only one of them is declared\n\n  * `presents_as` \u2014 what the deformation CLAIMS to serve. A claim, supplied.\n  * `serves_*`    \u2014 whom it ACTUALLY serves. NOT declared: measured, by auditing\n                    the deformed continuation against each frame's OWN tube.\n                    A deformation serves whoever's tube it keeps you inside.\n\nManipulation is the divergence between them, which is why one field cannot express\nit. The mismatch is the phenomenon.\n\nThis computes a comparison. It does not compute an ethical verdict, and the rule\nthat flags divergence is named for what it measures rather than for what a reader\nmight conclude from it.\n",
